   "source": [
    "#fillin\n",
    "plt.hist(Train['AtBat'])\n",
    "plt.xlabel('At Bat')\n",
    "plt.ylabel('Frequency')\n",
    "plt.title('Histogram of At Bat')\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "407b82a2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Let's standardize the AtBat feature (i.e., normalize by z-scores). We'll create a new column in the training data called 'AtBat_st' to represent this."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 18,
   "id": "6dc0fac0",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#fillin\n",
    "Train['AtBat_st'] = (Train['AtBat'] - Train['AtBat'].mean()) / Train['AtBat'].std()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ae45cc7e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### How many rows have an 'AtBat' value within the first standard deviation?\n",
    "\n",
    "Hint: the 'len' magic method returns the number of rows of a dataFrame."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 19,
   "id": "a9f8e3c9",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "115"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 19,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "#fillin\n",
    "len(Train[(Train['AtBat_st'] >= -1) & (Train['AtBat_st'] <= 1)])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cb7ec07f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Going back to the results of the 'describe' method, how can you tell that the 'Salary' variable has missing values?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cf0fb15c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Because the desribe function in the Training subset shows the amount of data it has, known as count. For every other variable, the count is 193, but for salary it only has 164. Therefore it is missing 29 values compared to all the other variables."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3de13827",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Describe a situation where a variable could have missing values but this would not be reflected in the results of the 'describe' method."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6513b00a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "It could be that in a particular dataset, that instead of putting nothing into a variable if there was no collected data, it might automatically set it to default value, such as 0 or 1. This would mean the count in the describe function would be the same for all variables, but that doesn't mean that their is actually useful data in it, or it was missing and a placeholder value was implemented. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "10dcf6b6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### On the training data, create separate boxplots of the 'AtBat' variable for when 'Salary' is populated or missing."
   ]
  },
