   "source": [
    "### Propose two different ways of imputing the missing values of Salary while taking advantage of the information given in the boxplots or the correlation matrix."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4074a08e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "one way could be using the mean of Atbat with those with a salary. It could be interpreted that a player of less recognition may not be studied as much , therefore a lost record of salaray. Applying the average salary allows for no favortism and no large assumptions. Another way could be since that the carrer features seem to be heavily linked with salary, shown in the matrix, you could do a regression-based imputation to estimate missing Salary values."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2d9927f3",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### For our last exercise, we'll explore Hits and Walks relative to AtBat totals. \n",
    "- Use the sum function to calculuate the totals of each of these three variables for the 1986 season (on the training set). \n",
    "- Create a pie chart which shows total hits, total walks, and remaining total (neither) as percents of the At Bats total (on the training set). "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 28,
   "id": "6ab5ba2f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "TotHits = Train['Hits'].sum()\n",
    "TotWalks = Train['Walks'].sum()\n",
    "TotAtBat = Train['AtBat'].sum()\n",
    "\n",
    "Labels = ['Hits', 'Walks', 'Neither']\n",
    "Totals = [TotHits, TotWalks, TotAtBat-TotHits-TotWalks]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 29,
   "id": "ac7743dc",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# pie chart\n",
    "# Define a range of colors\n",
    "colors = plt.get_cmap('Reds')(np.linspace(0.7, 0.2, len(Totals)))\n",
    "\n",
    "# Create a figure and axes\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Create the pie chart\n",
    "ax.pie(Totals, colors=colors, radius=3, center=(4, 4),\n",
    "       wedgeprops={\"linewidth\": 1, \"edgecolor\": \"black\"}, frame=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Include a legend\n",
    "ax.legend(Labels, loc=\"right\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Adjust the axes\n",
    "ax.set(xlim=(0, 12), xticks=np.arange(1, 8),\n",
    "       ylim=(0, 9), yticks=np.arange(1, 8))\n",
    "ax.tick_params(left = False, labelleft = False, \n",
    "               bottom = False, labelbottom = False)\n",
    "ax.set(title=\"Pie of Pies\")\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "387cb24b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### The previous two cells gave us totals across all players. For each player in the training set, calculate the Hits as a percent of AtBat and store it in a new variable called 'AVG'"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 30,
   "id": "040fbabb",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#fillin\n",
    "Train['AVG'] = Train['Hits'] / Train['AtBat']"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "707d9726",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Using 0.25 and 0.31 as the split points, create a new variable with three bins: high, medium, and low. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 35,
   "id": "50f1f78c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "Train['AVG_bin'] = 'medium'\n",
    "Train.loc[Train['AVG'] <= 0.25, 'AVG_bin'] = 'low'\n",
    "Train.loc[Train['AVG'] >= 0.31, 'AVG_bin'] = 'high'"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7fcd6b57",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Create a bar chart that displays the number of players in each of the low, medium, and high categories (for the training data)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 36,
   "id": "bcf402b4",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "AVG_bin\n",
       "medium    110\n",
       "low        69\n",
       "high       14\n",
       "Name: count, dtype: int64"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 36,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "Train['AVG_bin'].value_counts()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a51a01be",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Notice that the order of the bars will be medium, low, high. That's counterintuitive. We can reorder these quickly. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 37,
   "id": "bf54b862",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "indexMap = ['low', 'medium', 'high']\n",
    "reordered_list = [Train['AVG_bin'].value_counts()[i] for i in indexMap]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 40,
   "id": "8d54c022",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "plt.bar(range(len(reordered_list)), reordered_list)\n",
    "plt.title(\"1986 AVG (Training Set)\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Number of Players\")\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.xticks(range(len(indexMap)), indexMap)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cd172204",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Did we use the depth method or width method for creating these bins? Explain."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0476cc83",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This is the width method because we choose clear cut of points based by us. We see that each bin does not have equal number of players so it could not be depth method. The content of the bins is determined by our selected AVG thresholds."
   ]
  }
